\f1\fs22 \

\f3\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Who was this loon?
\f1\i0  Sarah wondered. No time at all elapsed before she identified the celebrity host of 
\f3\i Candid Criminal
\f1\i0 . In a fit of raging disgust, she put the day\'92s supply of vibrators among the muffins. The teddy bears and bunny rabbits quaked with fright. She cursed Alexander\'92s name. There was only one man who rescued her from the bombing: Mr Fairy Boxers.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah required none of the advanced medicines and procedures available to her. Astonishingly, she recovered from her injuries within two days. Rather than investigate her miraculous recovery, the doctors, nurses, bathroom attendants, and professorial boffins simply chalked it up to the superior genes of the Airborne, pondering aloud if eugenics hadn\'92t been abandoned rather too early.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony regained consciousness, significantly ignored and untreated, in a sweaty bed in a maze of bacterially alarming smudges. He grimaced at the state of decay of the large ward; the greys were too green and yellow, the greens and yellows were too brown, and the browns were too much like the vomit he coughed up when he registered how much his skull hurt. The nurses gave him dusty pills the size of party pies and told him to rest.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs22 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Overcome by the pungent scents of Alexander\'92s flower baskets, Sarah checked herself out early. She felt fully recovered anyway, and was anxious to thank Tony.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>When she climbed the fire-escape stairs and entered the ward, she almost gagged with the stench. However, she was determined to press on.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Can I help you?\'92 the orderly at the ward desk asked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I doubt it.\'92 Sarah strode past the orderly with authority. Among the endless rows of paint-chipped bed-frames, saggy mattresses and saggy patients, she almost passed Tony without recognition.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Sarah!\'92 he called out.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>She gasped when he held her gaze with his unblinking right eye. His blackened left eye was bruised shut, and seeped a dilute blood-ooze that trickled over the lumps, cuts, scratches, and bruises that contrived something not too dissimilar to a face.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Oh, my tax accountants! What are they doing to you?\'92 She fled to his side and grasped his hand.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Ow!\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>She released his hand. \'91Are they even treating you?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91The doctors diagnosed it as acute sunburn.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What? But \'85 the cuts and bruises \'85 your eyebrows and most of your hair is singed off.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Shaving accident, they said.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91This is appalling!\'92 she sputtered. \'91I\'92ve never seen such \'85 
\f3\i squalor
\f1\i0  \'85\'92 
\f3\i Only a quick hover-copt jump from where I live
\f1\i0 , she thought, but could not bring herself to admit it.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You\'92ve never seen Cloudborne health care before, then? All they prescribe is sticking plaster and suppositories.\'92 Tony was about to remark that he had a better medical kit at home, but thought wiser of making such facts known in public spaces.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah straightened her spine with decision making. \'91You\'92re coming with me,\'92 she said. \'91He\'92s coming with me,\'92 she announced to the ward.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You can do better, dearie,\'92 said a withered nurse stumbling by on a cane.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Thank you, that would be most kind,\'92 Tony said to Sarah.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I need a wheelchair,\'92 Sarah demanded.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The withered nurse cackled.\
\pard\pardeftab360\sl360\slmult1\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 <!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 Sarah returned a half-hour later, her fingers tingling with the excitement of her thievery, having purloined a wheelchair from an Airborne hospital. Tony sat in it, and fortified himself with admirable resolve and tenacity. Sarah wheeled him to the stairwell, where they met two orderlies she paid to carry Tony-in-wheelchair to the ground floor. From there, she pushed and shoved across unreliable ground to the nearest hover-pad stalk, where she and Tony made their escape.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>They made most of the journey to Tony\'92s apartment discreetly by Cloudborne ground cab, which served to enhance Sarah\'92s experience and understanding of the deteriorating Cloudborne lifestyle. Everything she saw was worn-down, broken, taped, jury-rigged, bent to shape, chipped, corroded, smeared, or tied up with string. \'91These are are a few of my ghastliest things,\'92 Sarah sang under her breath. The seats of the groundcab were torn and exploded springs. The doors to the back seat were clearly from another model of vehicle entirely, and didn\'92t shut properly. Gaps between the doors and wheel arches allowed Sarah to see through to the street. The steering wheel was almost completely missing. The driver steered by grasping the stubs of the steering wheel spokes. She braked by stomping on the floor like she was exterminating cockroaches. She accelerated by rocking back and forward in her seat, as if that would quicken the tired vehicle\'92s pace.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>When they reached Tony\'92s front door, he presented his armpit, but his door stayed resolutely shut.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91It didn\'92t even read me,\'92 he complained.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You might have to stand up,\'92 Sarah said. She helped him to his feet and supported him while he lifted his arm again. The door clicked open, and Tony slumped into the wheelchair.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Not exactly disability-friendly,\'92 Tony said solemnly.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91No,\'92 Sarah agreed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah was dismayed by the size and condition of Tony\'92s living space, but did her best to hide it. Tony wheeled himself to his room, bundled onto the bed, and passed out.\
\pard\pardeftab360\sl360\slmult1\partightenfactor0

