\f0 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 Over the next few days, Sarah devoted her spare time and spare thoughts to Tony. She risked the trip to his apartment twice a day so she could feed, clothe, and bathe him (boxer shorts resolutely stayed on), and tend to his wounds with Airborne bandages, creams, unguents, and toenail clippers. She filled his stasis fridge with microwave meals. She bought him new socks, cologne and vodka he could actually drink. She subscribed him to the best on-demand Xtreme Fighting Porn, and he marvelled at the tag-team doggy hammer throws. Although scars remained encamped on his face, hands and chest, his health and demeanour improved. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>One night, Sarah was folding Tony\'92s laundry as he sat gingerly on the couch, his skin still taught and tender, and watched some streams.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I don\'92t know why you don\'92t throw these out, Mr Fairy Boxers,\'92 Sarah said as she held up his threadbare underwear.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I like them,\'92 Tony said defensively.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I\'92ll get you some new ones.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony scoffed. \'91I got those in Floormart. An Airborne would never be caught dead in a Floormart.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah shrugged. \'91I\'92ll send someone then. My cleaner does odd jobs for me; he\'92ll do it.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Then 
\f3\i he 
\f1\i0 might be caught dead there.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah\'92s turn to scoff: \'91That\'92s urban myth; no one\'92s ever died in a stock delivery accident.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Despite his discomfort, Tony sat up straight and leant forward. \'91Have you ever seen a Floormart?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91It\'92s a department store; how bad can it be?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Do you even know why they call it Floormart?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah arrested her shaking head, tried to make it not but only succeeded in making a non-committal shrug and a guilty smile.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91There are no shelves in there, that\'92s why. All the merch is just dumped on the floor. \'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Okay, I get it: it\'92s low-rent. But that doesn\'92t mean people die in them.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony had forgotten his burns. \'91Floormarts are just open basements with tin roofs. They stock them by backing tip-trucks up and dumping the delivery straight in. They just let it pile up where it may. It\'92s a shopper\'92s problem to stay out of the way, in case they end up at the bottom of one of those piles. I was once looking for a packet of fusilli in the dry food section and found a body at the bottom of a pasta mountain. Whatever you do, don\'92t go near a Floormart.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>When Sarah felt his wounds were settled enough, she thought it would improve his appearance if she could exfoliate all the flaking dead skin away. One night she sat him on bath\'92s edge, naked but for his boxer shorts, and produced a purple-black cloth which she used to buff his head.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Ow!\'92 Tony complained.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Men are such babies,\'92 Sarah scolded. \'91Look, it\'92s working.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony checked out his noggin in the mirror, and was pleased with the result. He rubbed his hand over his scalp and enjoyed the smoother feel where Sarah had worked. He submitted to her resumption. \'91What is that cloth anyway?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Giraffe tongue.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony pushed her away. \'91What the actual tax? Aren\'92t they extinct?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Well they are now,\'92 Sarah said as she rubbed behind his ears.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Great. You\'92re a giraffe exterminator. Is there anything you Airborne won\'92t kill?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Well, I didn\'92t kill it. My grandmother left me this when I was a child. It has great sentimental value.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Really?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Yeah. She used to polish her bowling balls with it.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>When Sarah finished, Tony proudly modelled for her. She applauded. He felt reinvigorated.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Hey, I\'92m gonna take a shower. What\'92s say we go out for a couple of drinks? I\'92m dying to splash-on some of that new vodka you got me.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah looked at him askance. \'91Are you asking me on a date?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony blushed and shrugged. \'91Nah, just some karaoke, you know \'85\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91At an Elvi bar?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Of course. What other kind is there?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah beamed as fully as if he asked her on a weekend riverboat cruise.
